1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,870
On April the eighteenth, 2018, in a store
car park in Roseville, California,

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,090
a detective swabbed the door
handle of a parked car.

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:14,260
Five days later, another detective
took a used tissue from a trash

4
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:15,950
can outside the owner's house.

5
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:17,670
Both went to a lab.

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:23,710
They matched DNA left at the scene
of a double murder in 1980.

7
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:27,920
For thirty-eight years, that
DNA had matched no one.

8
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:31,920
The man it came from had never been
arrested, so he had never been

9
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:33,450
in any police database.

10
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:37,640
He wasn't found in a police database.

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,930
He was found through his relatives:
cousins who had sent their DNA

12
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:46,080
to genealogy websites to find out
where their families came from.

13
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:49,470
None of them was asked.

14
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,750
Most have never been named.

15
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,220
And before the search found the right
man, it pointed at the wrong

16
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:56,690
one.

17
00:00:59,610 --> 00:01:01,910
The man was the Golden State Killer.

18
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:07,780
Between the mid-1970s and 1986, he
murdered thirteen people in California

19
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,990
and attacked dozens more.

20
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:12,750
In 2020 he pleaded guilty.

21
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:15,130
He is serving life without parole.

22
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:19,820
His arrest made the method famous.

23
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:24,560
Since then, police have used it in
well over a thousand cases.

24
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:28,300
And you don't have to take a DNA
test to be found by it.

25
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:29,840
A cousin is enough.

26
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:34,710
So, should police be allowed to
search your family's DNA?

27
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:39,570
When one person shares their DNA, they
share part of their relatives'

28
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,030
DNA too. Who gets to say
yes for a family?

29
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,310
And what should the state be
allowed to do with the answer?

30
00:01:48,790 --> 00:01:51,960
We went through the court record, the
investigators' own accounts,

31
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:56,130
the science of how far a family tree
can reach, and the first laws

32
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:57,290
written to control it.

33
00:01:57,310 --> 00:02:01,660
At the end, we'll give you a verdict,
and tell you how sure we are.

34
00:02:13,565 --> 00:02:17,645
It began in the mid-1970s in Visalia,
in California's Central Valley,

35
00:02:17,665 --> 00:02:19,785
with more than a hundred burglaries.

36
00:02:19,805 --> 00:02:25,085
Then it moved north, to Sacramento and
the East Bay, and then south,

37
00:02:25,105 --> 00:02:29,275
to Santa Barbara, Ventura
and Orange counties.

38
00:02:29,905 --> 00:02:32,875
For years, police thought they were
looking for different men.

39
00:02:34,055 --> 00:02:35,745
They gave him different names.

40
00:02:36,175 --> 00:02:37,195
The Visalia Ransacker.

41
00:02:37,215 --> 00:02:39,315
The East Area Rapist.

42
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:41,315
The Original Night Stalker.

43
00:02:41,335 --> 00:02:45,245
DNA showed some of them
were the same man.

44
00:02:45,835 --> 00:02:50,155
The name most people know came later, in
2013, from the writer Michelle

45
00:02:50,175 --> 00:02:50,845
McNamara.

46
00:02:52,385 --> 00:02:55,055
The court record names
thirteen people he murdered.

47
00:02:55,665 --> 00:02:59,295
Claude Snelling, in Visalia, in 1975.

48
00:02:59,985 --> 00:03:03,485
Katie and Brian Maggiore, in
Rancho Cordova, in 1978.

49
00:03:03,505 --> 00:03:09,355
Debra Alexandria Manning and Robert
Offerman, in Goleta, in 1979.

50
00:03:09,685 --> 00:03:14,865
Charlene and Lyman Smith, in
Ventura, in 1980.

51
00:03:14,885 --> 00:03:19,685
Keith and Patrice Harrington, in
Dana Point, that summer.

52
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:23,465
Manuela Witthuhn, in Irvine, in 1981.

53
00:03:23,485 --> 00:03:29,145
Cheri Domingo and Gregory
Sanchez, in Goleta, that July.

54
00:03:29,165 --> 00:03:33,025
And Janelle Cruz, in Irvine, in 1986.

55
00:03:33,045 --> 00:03:35,235
She was eighteen.

56
00:03:39,335 --> 00:03:42,535
Claude Snelling was killed trying to
stop a stranger from taking his

57
00:03:42,555 --> 00:03:46,915
daughter. Keith and Patrice Harrington
never reached their first wedding

58
00:03:46,935 --> 00:03:50,605
anniversary. Janelle Cruz hoped
to graduate from college.

59
00:03:51,535 --> 00:03:55,165
Her sister, Michelle, told the court,
"Janelle was my big sister.

60
00:03:55,615 --> 00:03:57,265
She was one year older than me."

61
00:04:00,045 --> 00:04:01,075
Many more survived.

62
00:04:01,645 --> 00:04:05,645
In the end, prosecutors counted
eighty-seven victims, attacked on

63
00:04:05,665 --> 00:04:08,555
fifty-three separate
occasions, in eleven counties.

64
00:04:09,365 --> 00:04:12,635
The prosecutor told the court there
had been almost fifty rapes.

65
00:04:14,335 --> 00:04:17,485
Police had his DNA from
the crime scenes.

66
00:04:17,895 --> 00:04:19,405
What they didn't have was him.

67
00:04:20,195 --> 00:04:23,255
A crime-scene profile can be checked
against the national police DNA

68
00:04:23,275 --> 00:04:28,645
database, which in 2018 held more
than twelve million profiles.

69
00:04:29,055 --> 00:04:32,675
But it can only match someone who's
already in it, someone who's been

70
00:04:32,695 --> 00:04:34,045
arrested or convicted.

71
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:35,685
He never had been.

72
00:04:37,205 --> 00:04:41,625
In 2004, Keith Harrington's brother,
Bruce, sponsored a ballot measure

73
00:04:41,645 --> 00:04:45,765
to add everyone arrested for a felony
in California to the state's

74
00:04:45,785 --> 00:04:48,995
DNA database, and paid for
much of it himself.

75
00:04:49,965 --> 00:04:54,115
Voters passed it. It still couldn't find
a man who was never arrested.

76
00:04:55,715 --> 00:04:57,925
So, the first weight on the scale.

77
00:04:58,715 --> 00:05:03,275
Thirteen dead, dozens of survivors, and
families who waited four decades

78
00:05:03,295 --> 00:05:07,895
for a name. The tool the law had built
to find him couldn't, because

79
00:05:07,915 --> 00:05:10,285
it only looks where police
have already been.

80
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:20,940
In 2017, a team including Paul Holes,
a cold-case investigator in

81
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:24,340
Contra Costa County, and the
FBI tried something new.

82
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:29,440
They took DNA from the 1980 Smith
murders and turned it into the kind

83
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:32,990
of file that hobby genealogists
upload to find their cousins.

84
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:36,100
Their first lead was wrong.

85
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:39,570
A partial match on a genealogy site
pointed to a seventy-three-year-old

86
00:05:39,590 --> 00:05:43,630
man in a nursing home in Oregon.

87
00:05:43,650 --> 00:05:45,490
A judge ordered a sample.

88
00:05:45,510 --> 00:05:48,570
His daughter says nobody told the
family before he was swabbed in

89
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:51,720
his bed. He was only a very
distant relative.

90
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:56,110
Then they went to the consumer sites.

91
00:05:56,840 --> 00:06:01,100
FamilyTreeDNA, a commercial testing
company, let them search its customers

92
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:02,200
under a made-up account.

93
00:06:02,220 --> 00:06:07,790
GEDmatch, a free site where people
upload their DNA files, didn't

94
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:09,130
know it was being used.

95
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:15,430
And in early 2018, the genealogist on
the team, Barbara Rae-Venter,

96
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:20,090
uploaded the DNA to MyHeritage on her
own account, without telling

97
00:06:20,110 --> 00:06:24,780
the company. None of the customers on
any of these sites was told.

98
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,110
Which site cracked it is
still disputed.

99
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:33,310
The first accounts in 2018
credited GEDmatch.

100
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:38,300
A Los Angeles Times investigation in
2020 found the closer matches

101
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:39,850
came from MyHeritage.

102
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:44,240
Either way, the search ran on the DNA
of people who had shared it

103
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:45,350
for a different reason.

104
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:49,970
A second cousin shares a pair of
great-grandparents with you.

105
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:54,430
From matches like that, the team built
family trees back to shared

106
00:06:54,450 --> 00:06:57,660
ancestors, then worked forward
through their descendants, looking

107
00:06:58,290 --> 00:07:01,420
for a man of the right age who had
been in the right places.

108
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:04,980
At one point the prime suspect
was a cousin's brother.

109
00:07:05,290 --> 00:07:08,160
She gave her own DNA, and
it cleared him.

110
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:10,640
In the end, six men fit.

111
00:07:10,930 --> 00:07:13,620
One had blue eyes, which
matched the profile.

112
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:19,960
He was a former police officer,
seventy-two years old, living in Citrus

113
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:21,850
Heights, outside Sacramento.

114
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,170
Detectives watched him for
about ten days.

115
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,870
The swab from his car door
matched the 1980 DNA.

116
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:31,330
So did the tissue from his trash.

117
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:37,630
On April the twenty-fourth, 2018,
Joseph James DeAngelo was arrested

118
00:07:37,650 --> 00:07:40,860
at his home. It's the only
time we'll say his name.

119
00:07:41,410 --> 00:07:45,760
The Sacramento district attorney, Anne
Marie Schubert, told reporters,

120
00:07:46,210 --> 00:07:49,780
"We found the needle in the haystack, and
it was right here in Sacramento."

121
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:53,150
There was no trial.

122
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:58,920
In June 2020, in a university ballroom
in Sacramento, he pleaded guilty

123
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:02,820
to thirteen murders and thirteen
kidnappings, and admitted dozens

124
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:05,470
of crimes he could no
longer be charged with.

125
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:08,850
In return, prosecutors
dropped the death penalty.

126
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:12,440
They said they had consulted the
victims and their families, and that

127
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:14,430
a trial could have taken ten years.

128
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:18,630
That August, survivors and families
spoke in court for three days.

129
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:22,490
He was sentenced to eleven
consecutive life terms without parole.

130
00:08:24,570 --> 00:08:27,780
For a long time, how he was
found stayed secret.

131
00:08:28,530 --> 00:08:31,790
At first, prosecutors wouldn't say
which sites had been used.

132
00:08:31,810 --> 00:08:37,050
In 2019, a prosecutor on the case in
Ventura County wrote to the FBI

133
00:08:37,070 --> 00:08:42,100
that keeping it quiet perpetuated, in her
words, a fraudulent impression.

134
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:44,120
She didn't doubt it was legal.

135
00:08:44,490 --> 00:08:46,300
Her objection was the secrecy.

136
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:49,410
So, the second weight.

137
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:53,960
It worked. A man who had escaped for
forty years was found in months,

138
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:56,650
and the families heard him
admit what he did.

139
00:08:57,220 --> 00:09:00,1000
But the people whose DNA made it work
were never asked, the first

140
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:05,440
lead fell on an innocent old man, and
for years the public wasn't

141
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:06,630
told how it was done.

142
00:09:12,749 --> 00:09:14,159
The arrest opened the door.

143
00:09:14,349 --> 00:09:17,909
By one count, at least thirteen more
cases were solved the same way

144
00:09:17,929 --> 00:09:19,139
in the next four months.

145
00:09:19,909 --> 00:09:24,699
By early 2024, a researcher who tracks
them, Tracey Leigh Dowdeswell,

146
00:09:24,929 --> 00:09:26,359
counted more than eleven hundred.

147
00:09:27,189 --> 00:09:29,479
Most involved sexual
violence or murder.

148
00:09:30,009 --> 00:09:31,879
Some gave names back to the dead.

149
00:09:33,519 --> 00:09:38,309
In 2018, a team of scientists, two of
them working for MyHeritage,

150
00:09:38,679 --> 00:09:40,309
measured how far this reaches.

151
00:09:40,919 --> 00:09:45,019
Using more than a million profiles,
they estimated that about sixty

152
00:09:45,039 --> 00:09:48,559
percent of searches for someone of
European descent would turn up

153
00:09:48,579 --> 00:09:50,309
a third cousin or closer.

154
00:09:50,799 --> 00:09:53,249
That's usually enough to
start building a tree.

155
00:09:53,879 --> 00:09:58,009
A database covering just two percent of
a population, they calculated,

156
00:09:58,259 --> 00:10:00,509
could reach nearly anyone in it.

157
00:10:01,869 --> 00:10:03,339
The reach isn't even.

158
00:10:03,889 --> 00:10:07,129
People of Northern European background
were thirty percent more likely

159
00:10:07,149 --> 00:10:10,269
to have a close match than people of
sub-Saharan African background,

160
00:10:10,289 --> 00:10:13,699
because more of their
relatives have tested.

161
00:10:14,389 --> 00:10:19,429
Police DNA databases skew the other way:
Black Americans are over-represented

162
00:10:19,449 --> 00:10:24,229
in them. Some scholars argue the
genealogy sites might begin to balance

163
00:10:24,249 --> 00:10:25,059
that out.

164
00:10:26,819 --> 00:10:28,649
And the rules kept moving.

165
00:10:29,479 --> 00:10:33,359
After the arrest, GEDmatch changed its
terms to allow police searches

166
00:10:33,379 --> 00:10:34,729
for murder and rape.

167
00:10:35,339 --> 00:10:38,799
Months later, its founder made an
exception for an assault in Utah.

168
00:10:38,819 --> 00:10:43,969
After an outcry, in May 2019,
users had to opt in.

169
00:10:44,399 --> 00:10:47,569
Overnight, the profiles open to
police fell to zero.

170
00:10:48,419 --> 00:10:51,859
Two months later, a judge in Florida
gave a detective a warrant to

171
00:10:51,879 --> 00:10:55,429
search the whole database, including
the people who had opted out.

172
00:10:55,979 --> 00:10:59,779
Then the site was sold to a forensic
genetics company, which was itself

173
00:10:59,799 --> 00:11:01,329
bought four years later.

174
00:11:02,589 --> 00:11:06,229
The law professor Erin Murphy said of
that warrant that it was a signal

175
00:11:06,249 --> 00:11:08,699
that no genetic
information can be safe.

176
00:11:10,399 --> 00:11:12,749
Other companies promise more.

177
00:11:13,379 --> 00:11:16,409
Ancestry says it insists on a
warrant or court order.

178
00:11:17,019 --> 00:11:20,729
23andMe says it has never handed any
customer's data to police.

179
00:11:21,479 --> 00:11:23,389
But promises depend on the company.

180
00:11:23,979 --> 00:11:28,699
In 2023, hackers got into about
fourteen thousand 23andMe accounts

181
00:11:28,719 --> 00:11:32,939
and, through a feature that links
relatives, exposed the profiles

182
00:11:32,959 --> 00:11:35,309
of six point nine million people.

183
00:11:35,939 --> 00:11:41,379
In 2025, the company went bankrupt
and was sold, genetic data and

184
00:11:41,399 --> 00:11:41,829
all.

185
00:11:43,229 --> 00:11:44,759
So, the third weight.

186
00:11:45,409 --> 00:11:47,339
You don't have to take a
test to be found.

187
00:11:47,749 --> 00:11:51,039
If enough of your cousins
have, you already can be.

188
00:11:51,449 --> 00:11:55,609
And the rules on when police may look
have been written, and rewritten,

189
00:11:55,629 --> 00:11:58,399
by whoever happens to own the data.

190
00:12:04,770 --> 00:12:06,280
One case shows both edges.

191
00:12:06,650 --> 00:12:09,960
In 1996, Angie Dodge was
murdered in Idaho Falls.

192
00:12:10,310 --> 00:12:13,860
A young man, Christopher Tapp, was
convicted after a false confession.

193
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:19,270
In 2014, police searched a genealogy
database owned by Ancestry, without

194
00:12:19,290 --> 00:12:23,510
a warrant, and found a near match: the
father of a filmmaker named

195
00:12:23,530 --> 00:12:28,720
Michael Usry. Usry was swabbed and waited
a month before he was cleared.

196
00:12:29,510 --> 00:12:31,720
Angie's mother, Carol, kept pushing.

197
00:12:32,290 --> 00:12:37,370
In 2019, genetic genealogy found the
real killer, and Tapp's conviction

198
00:12:37,390 --> 00:12:41,730
was thrown out. It's believed to be the
first exoneration by genetic

199
00:12:41,750 --> 00:12:42,660
genealogy.

200
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:46,810
Searching for relatives wasn't new.

201
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:51,920
Since 2008, California has let its lab
look for partial matches in

202
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:55,740
the state's own police database,
pointing to a close relative of an

203
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:59,460
offender. That's how police found the
serial killer known as the Grim

204
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:02,170
Sleeper in 2010, through his son.

205
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:04,750
Maryland bans that kind of search.

206
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:08,540
But a police database only reaches
close relatives of people already

207
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:12,380
in the system. Genealogy sites reach
third cousins who have never

208
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:13,610
met a police officer.

209
00:13:15,010 --> 00:13:19,140
In 2019, the Justice Department wrote
rules for federal investigators.

210
00:13:20,010 --> 00:13:24,010
Genetic genealogy mainly for homicides
and sex crimes, and only after

211
00:13:24,030 --> 00:13:25,820
the police database has failed.

212
00:13:26,650 --> 00:13:30,670
Investigators must identify
themselves, and may only use sites that

213
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:35,220
warn their users. No one may be
arrested on a genetic link alone.

214
00:13:35,730 --> 00:13:38,970
Going by the Los Angeles Times
account, the search that found the

215
00:13:38,990 --> 00:13:41,280
Golden State Killer would have
broken those rules.

216
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:44,810
Then came the first state laws.

217
00:13:45,620 --> 00:13:50,580
Maryland, in 2021: a judge must approve
every search, police may only

218
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:54,660
use sites that ask their users'
consent, and if a relative refuses

219
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:57,970
to give a sample, police
can't secretly take one.

220
00:13:58,540 --> 00:14:02,110
Montana, the same year: a
warrant or a court order.

221
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:07,840
Utah, in 2023: only for violent
felonies and missing or unidentified

222
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:08,550
people.

223
00:14:10,270 --> 00:14:12,040
The courts have mostly let it stand.

224
00:14:12,550 --> 00:14:17,250
In 2023, an appeals court in
Washington State put it bluntly: there

225
00:14:17,270 --> 00:14:21,110
is no privacy interest in DNA you
share with a relative who uploads

226
00:14:21,130 --> 00:14:24,780
it voluntarily. The Supreme
Court hasn't ruled on it.

227
00:14:25,050 --> 00:14:29,090
Its closest decision, two months after
the arrest, said police need

228
00:14:29,110 --> 00:14:32,000
a warrant for months of your
phone's location records.

229
00:14:32,630 --> 00:14:34,020
It didn't mention DNA.

230
00:14:34,670 --> 00:14:37,480
And taking his trash needed
no warrant at all.

231
00:14:37,770 --> 00:14:39,960
The court decided that in 1988.

232
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,870
Most people accept the narrow version.

233
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:48,400
In a 2018 survey, eighty percent
supported police using these sites

234
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:48,940
for violent crimes.

235
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:51,830
For nonviolent crimes,
thirty-nine percent.

236
00:14:54,610 --> 00:14:56,660
So, the fourth weight.

237
00:14:57,330 --> 00:15:01,800
Every rule we found, from the Justice
Department, from Maryland, Montana

238
00:15:01,970 --> 00:15:06,570
and Utah, even from the companies,
draws its lines in the same three

239
00:15:06,590 --> 00:15:12,230
places: which crimes, who has to
agree, and whether a judge signs

240
00:15:12,250 --> 00:15:16,020
off. The real argument isn't
whether to do this.

241
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:18,340
It's where those lines go.

242
00:15:24,348 --> 00:15:27,958
First, the strongest case for
letting police search.

243
00:15:28,608 --> 00:15:33,358
Thirteen people were murdered and dozens
attacked, and for forty years

244
00:15:33,528 --> 00:15:34,658
the law had nothing.

245
00:15:35,228 --> 00:15:39,238
This found him, and it has solved more
than a thousand cases since.

246
00:15:39,708 --> 00:15:44,018
It has freed an innocent man and
given names back to the dead.

247
00:15:44,848 --> 00:15:49,648
The people who uploaded weren't forced:
they chose to share, and now

248
00:15:49,668 --> 00:15:51,178
they have to opt in.

249
00:15:51,548 --> 00:15:54,338
A cousin can already tell
the police about you.

250
00:15:54,948 --> 00:15:57,538
Why should a cousin's
DNA be different?

251
00:15:59,158 --> 00:16:00,988
Now the strongest case against.

252
00:16:01,818 --> 00:16:03,508
You can't opt out of your relatives.

253
00:16:03,978 --> 00:16:08,038
When a cousin uploads, part of your
genome goes with it, and you were

254
00:16:08,058 --> 00:16:12,948
never asked. The rules have changed again
and again after people uploaded.

255
00:16:13,458 --> 00:16:17,878
The data has been breached and sold,
and one warrant overrode a whole

256
00:16:17,898 --> 00:16:19,668
database's privacy settings.

257
00:16:19,978 --> 00:16:23,008
The method has already
fallen on innocent men.

258
00:16:23,678 --> 00:16:27,398
And once police can search for murder,
the pressure is always to search

259
00:16:27,418 --> 00:16:32,198
for less. A power like this is easier to
keep narrow before it spreads

260
00:16:32,218 --> 00:16:33,088
than after.

261
00:16:34,228 --> 00:16:35,038
Here's our verdict.

262
00:16:35,828 --> 00:16:39,238
On the Golden State Killer:
catching him this way was right.

263
00:16:39,648 --> 00:16:44,198
Doing it in secret, on sites that
hadn't warned their users, was not.

264
00:16:45,008 --> 00:16:46,488
Thirteen murders justify the search.

265
00:16:46,508 --> 00:16:48,508
They don't justify hiding it.

266
00:16:48,528 --> 00:16:51,248
Our confidence is eighty percent.

267
00:16:51,268 --> 00:16:55,368
What would change our mind: evidence
that being open about the search

268
00:16:55,388 --> 00:16:57,638
at the time would have let him escape.

269
00:17:01,478 --> 00:17:06,258
On the police: yes, they should be
allowed, but only for murder and

270
00:17:06,278 --> 00:17:10,878
rape, with a judge's approval each
time, and only on sites that ask

271
00:17:10,898 --> 00:17:15,278
their users first. Our confidence
is seventy-five percent.

272
00:17:15,298 --> 00:17:19,938
What would change our mind: evidence
that limits like these don't

273
00:17:19,958 --> 00:17:24,118
hold in practice, that the searches
still spread to lesser crimes

274
00:17:24,138 --> 00:17:26,768
or keep falling on innocent relatives.

275
00:17:30,548 --> 00:17:32,098
And the bigger question.

276
00:17:33,208 --> 00:17:37,478
When one person shares their DNA,
who consents for the family?

277
00:17:38,028 --> 00:17:41,258
Our verdict: no one can, fully.

278
00:17:42,028 --> 00:17:45,448
So the limits shouldn't be left to
whoever owns the database this

279
00:17:45,468 --> 00:17:47,678
year. They should be set by law.

280
00:17:48,208 --> 00:17:52,328
Our confidence is seventy percent,
lower, because laws can be weak,

281
00:17:52,348 --> 00:17:56,478
and some companies have protected their
users better than any statute.

282
00:17:57,428 --> 00:18:01,448
What would change our mind: evidence
that company rules have held

283
00:18:01,468 --> 00:18:05,748
steadier, and protected people better,
than the laws written to replace

284
00:18:05,768 --> 00:18:06,418
them.

285
00:18:09,918 --> 00:18:11,068
That's our verdict.

286
00:18:11,718 --> 00:18:15,408
It's open, which means it can change.

287
00:18:16,018 --> 00:18:19,248
If you think we got it wrong, make
your case in the comments.

288
00:18:19,738 --> 00:18:22,508
We'll answer the strongest one
in the next episode.

289
00:18:26,078 --> 00:18:30,758
Next case: in 1989, five teenagers in
New York were accused of an

290
00:18:30,778 --> 00:18:32,068
attack in Central Park.

291
00:18:32,898 --> 00:18:34,088
Four confessed on video.

292
00:18:34,938 --> 00:18:38,848
Thirteen years later, DNA and another
man's confession cleared them.

293
00:18:39,518 --> 00:18:41,988
Should police be allowed to lie to
the people they question?
